23341:
Gravitational work is W_{g} = mgh = 10^{-3} \times 10 \times 1000
This gives W_{g} = 10\text{ J}
Gain in kinetic energy is \frac{1}{2}mv^{2} = \frac{1}{2} \times 10^{-3} \times 50^{2} = 1.25\text{ J}
By work-energy theorem W_{g} + W_{air} = 1.25\text{ J}
So W_{air} = 1.25 - 10 = -8.75\text{ J}, which is option A
→ (A)

23342:
Magnetic moment is M = NIA
Area A = 2.1 \times 1.25\text{ cm}^{2} = 2.625 \times 10^{-4}\text{ m}^{2}
So M = 250 \times 85 \times 10^{-6} \times 2.625 \times 10^{-4}\text{ A m}^{2}
Work for 180^{\circ} rotation is W = 2MB
This gives W \approx 9.1 \mu\text{J}, which is option B
→ (B)

23343:
Unpolarised I_{0} through P_{1} gives \frac{I_{0}}{2}
P_{3} is at 45^{\circ} to P_{1}, so Malus gives \frac{I_{0}}{2}\cos^{2}45^{\circ} = \frac{I_{0}}{4}
P_{2} is perpendicular to P_{1}, so it sits 45^{\circ} from P_{3}
Malus again gives \frac{I_{0}}{4}\cos^{2}45^{\circ} = \frac{I_{0}}{8}
Hence the transmitted intensity is \frac{I_{0}}{8}, which is option B
→ (B)

23344:
N_{A} = N_{0} e^{-8\lambda t} and N_{B} = N_{0} e^{-\lambda t}
Ratio \frac{N_{A}}{N_{B}} = e^{-7\lambda t}
Set e^{-7\lambda t} = \frac{1}{e} = e^{-1}
Then 7\lambda t = 1 gives t = \frac{1}{7\lambda}
This value \frac{1}{7\lambda} is option C
→ (C)

23347:
Collector current is I_{c} = \frac{3}{3 \times 10^{3}} = 10^{-3}\text{ A}
Base current is I_{b} = \frac{I_{c}}{\beta} = 10^{-5}\text{ A}
Input voltage is I_{b}R_{b} = 10^{-5} \times 2 \times 10^{3} = 0.02\text{ V}
Voltage gain is \frac{3}{0.02} = 150 and power gain is 150 \times 100 = 15000
Hence 150 and 15000, which is option D
→ (D)

23348:
Source and observer move towards each other, so frequency rises
Apparent frequency is f^{\prime} = f\frac{v + v_{o}}{v - v_{s}}
Substituting gives 400 \times \frac{340 + 16.5}{340 - 22} = 400 \times \frac{356.5}{318}
This equals about 448\text{ Hz}, which is option A
→ (A)

23352:
Solenoid field change is \Delta B = \mu_{0}n\Delta I
Flux change per turn is \Delta B \times \pi r^{2}
Total charge is Q = \frac{N\Delta\Phi}{R}
Using \mu_{0} = 4\pi \times 10^{-7} this gives 32 \times 10^{-6}\text{ C}
Hence 32 \mu\text{C}, which is option D
→ (D)

23353:
Torque about the axis is \tau = FR
Moment of inertia of a hollow cylinder is I = MR^{2}
Angular acceleration is \alpha = \frac{\tau}{I} = \frac{F}{MR}
Substituting gives \frac{30}{3 \times 0.4} = 25\text{ rad/s}^{2}, which is option D
→ (D)

23354:
Initial energy is U_{0} = \frac{Q^{2}}{2C}
Charge Q is shared equally, so each capacitor carries \frac{Q}{2}
Final energy is 2 \times \frac{(Q/2)^{2}}{2C} = \frac{Q^{2}}{4C} = \frac{U_{0}}{2}
Hence the energy decreases by a factor of 2, which is option C
→ (C)

23355:
Above Earth g(h) = g(1 - \frac{2h}{R}) and below Earth g(d) = g(1 - \frac{d}{R})
Equating them gives \frac{2h}{R} = \frac{d}{R}
With h = 1\text{ km}, d = 2h = 2\text{ km}
This value d = 2\text{ km} is option C
→ (C)

23356:
Velocity and acceleration in SHM are v = \omega\sqrt{A^{2} - x^{2}} and a = \omega^{2}x
At x = 2 cm with A = 3 cm, |v| = |a| gives \omega\sqrt{5} = 2\omega^{2}
So \omega = \frac{\sqrt{5}}{2}
T = \frac{2\pi}{\omega} = \frac{4\pi}{\sqrt{5}}, which is option C
→ (C)

23358:
Photon energy is E = hc(\frac{1}{\lambda} - \frac{1}{\lambda_{0}}) \approx 1.07 \text{ eV}
Then \frac{1}{2}mv^{2} = E gives v \approx 6 \times 10^{5} \text{ m/s}
This is the same as 0.6 \times 10^{6} \text{ m/s}
Both values are options B and C
→ (B, C)

23359:
Each hydrogen atom carries net charge \Delta e
Coulomb repulsion \frac{1}{4\pi\varepsilon_{0}}\frac{(\Delta e)^{2}}{d^{2}} balances gravity \frac{Gm_{h}^{2}}{d^{2}}
So \Delta e = m_{h}\sqrt{\frac{G}{k}} with k = 9 \times 10^{9}
This gives \Delta e \approx 10^{-37} \text{ C}, which is option A
→ (A)

23360:
Force per unit length on B due to A is \frac{\mu_{0}I^{2}}{2\pi d}
Force per unit length on B due to C is also \frac{\mu_{0}I^{2}}{2\pi d}
The two forces act at right angles to each other
Resultant is \sqrt{2} times each, giving \frac{\mu_{0}I^{2}}{\sqrt{2}\pi d}, which is option B
→ (B)

23364:
Plausible dimensions give a length from the combination \frac{1}{c^{2}}[G\frac{e^{2}}{4\pi\varepsilon_{0}}]^{1/2}
This dimensional combination is option A
→ (A)

23365:
No deviation needs (\mu_{1} - 1)A_{1} = (\mu_{2} - 1)A_{2}
Here (1.42 - 1) \times 10^\circ = (1.7 - 1) \times A_{2}
So A_{2} = \frac{4.2}{0.7} = 6^\circ
The second prism angle 6^\circ is option C
→ (C)

23366:
Series limit follows \frac{1}{\lambda} = R \frac{1}{n_{1}^{2}}
Balmer limit n_{1} = 2 gives \lambda_{B} = \frac{4}{R}
Lyman limit n_{1} = 1 gives \lambda_{L} = \frac{1}{R}
Ratio \frac{\lambda_{B}}{\lambda_{L}} = 4, which is option D
→ (D)

23371:
Bulk modulus follows B = \frac{p}{(\Delta V / V)}
For a sphere V \propto R^{3}, giving \frac{\Delta V}{V} = \frac{3\Delta R}{R}
Hence \frac{\Delta R}{R} = \frac{p}{3B}, which is option B
→ (B)

23374:
Walking speed is \frac{L}{t_{1}} and escalator speed is \frac{L}{t_{2}}
Joint speed is \frac{L}{t_{1}} + \frac{L}{t_{2}}
Time is \frac{L}{v} = \frac{t_{1}t_{2}}{t_{1} + t_{2}}
This value \frac{t_{1}t_{2}}{t_{1} + t_{2}} is option D
→ (D)

23375:
Common speed from angular momentum is \omega = \frac{\omega_{1} + \omega_{2}}{2}
Initial energy is \frac{1}{2}I\omega_{1}^{2} + \frac{1}{2}I\omega_{2}^{2}
Final energy is I\omega^{2} with both discs locked together
Loss equals \frac{1}{4}I(\omega_{1} - \omega_{2})^{2}, which is option C
→ (C)

23378:
Peak electric field is E_{0} = \sqrt{2}E_{\text{rms}} = 6\sqrt{2}\text{ V/m}
Peak magnetic field follows B_{0} = \frac{E_{0}}{c}
Substitution gives B_{0} = 2.83 \times 10^{-8}\text{ T}, which is option C
→ (C)

23379:
Water rises 65\text{ mm} on one side and falls equally on the other
Difference of water levels is 2 \times 65 = 130\text{ mm}
Balancing oil column is 130 + 10 = 140\text{ mm}
Density \rho = 1000 \times \frac{130}{140} = 928\text{ kg m}^{-3}, which is option A
→ (A)

23381:
Thermal energy gives E = kT with p = \sqrt{2mkT}
Wavelength \lambda = \frac{h}{p} = \frac{h}{\sqrt{2mkT}}, which is option B
→ (B)

23403:
Leaching gives the soluble complex [Ag(CN)_{2}]^{-}
Zinc is more electropositive and displaces silver from this complex
2[Ag(CN)_{2}]^{-} + Zn gives 2Ag plus [Zn(CN)_{4}]^{2-}
So recovery is by displacement with Zn, which is option A
→ (A)

23411:
For first order k = \frac{2.303}{t}\log\frac{[A]_{0}}{[A]}
Here \frac{[A]_{0}}{[A]} = \frac{20}{5} = 4
t = \frac{2.303}{10^{-2}} \times 0.6020 = 138.6\text{ s}
Time 138.6 s is option C
→ (C)

23416:
E = E^{\ominus} - \frac{0.059}{2}\log\frac{[Zn^{2+}]}{[Cu^{2+}]}
E_{1} has ratio 10^{-2} while E_{2} has ratio 10^{2}
The larger log term makes E_{2} smaller, so E_{1} > E_{2}, which is option D
→ (D)

23422:
HgCl_{2} with excess I^{-} gives [HgI_{4}]^{2-} plus Cl^{-}
Dissolved I_{2} with I^{-} gives I_{3}^{-}
So the observed pair is [HgI_{4}]^{2-} and I_{3}^{-}, which is option D
→ (D)

23426:
Acidity rises with s character: sp > sp^{2} > sp^{3}
Alkyl groups donate electrons, so 	ext{CH}\equiv\text{CH} > CH_{3}-	ext{C}\equiv\text{CH}
Full order 	ext{CH}\equiv\text{CH} > CH_{3}-	ext{C}\equiv\text{CH} > 	ext{CH}_{2}=	ext{CH}_{2} > 	ext{CH}_{3}-	ext{CH}_{3}, which is option C
→ (C)
